1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,340
MATTHEW: Eight schools. Seven states. The Ivy League has trained presidents, justices, and a long line of governors. Tonight we are not going after the famous ones.

2
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:21,567
MATTHEW: Here is the game. Take the seven states that hold an Ivy League school. Count the governors from independence. Stop at number seven. Seven governors, seven minutes each, seven facts each. Lucky seven.

3
00:00:21,867 --> 00:00:27,533
MATTHEW: And every episode asks the same three questions. Who got a say. Who got left out. And who paid for it.

4
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:33,606
GARY MERCER: Matthew, I'm a Bowling Green guy. I know one governor from back east, and he was on the money.

5
00:00:33,906 --> 00:00:37,290
MATTHEW: Then you are going to learn six new ones, Gary. We start in Connecticut.

6
00:00:41,590 --> 00:00:53,293
MATTHEW: Philadelphia. The winter of 1798. The United States Congress meets in Congress Hall, next to the State House. It is a young country, and the men in this room still treat it like a gentlemen's club.

7
00:00:53,593 --> 00:01:12,901
MATTHEW: On January 30th, in a session on the House floor, Congressman Matthew Lyon of Vermont and Congressman Roger Griswold of Connecticut trade insults. Griswold asks him, mockingly, whether he'll be using his wooden sword, a jab at a disputed story that Lyon was drummed out of a command in the Revolution. Lyon spits tobacco juice in his face.

8
00:01:13,201 --> 00:01:26,297
MATTHEW: Two weeks later, February 15th, Griswold walks onto the floor with a hickory walking stick and goes after Lyon with it. Lyon grabs a pair of fireplace tongs from the hearth and fights back, right there, in the House of Representatives.

9
00:01:26,597 --> 00:01:29,497
GARY MERCER: Now hold on. Fireplace tongs? In Congress?

10
00:01:29,797 --> 00:01:33,971
THURSTON WHITMORE: Old boy, it was February. There was a fire. One makes do.

11
00:01:34,271 --> 00:01:45,795
MATTHEW: That is the man who would become Connecticut's seventh governor. And who he hit matters. Because Matthew Lyon was not a gentleman. Matthew Lyon had arrived in this country as a teenager who owed for his own passage.

12
00:01:46,095 --> 00:01:55,880
MATTHEW: Roger Griswold was born May 21st, 1762, in Lyme, Connecticut, at the mouth of the Connecticut River. He was born into the governing class, and not by accident.

13
00:01:56,180 --> 00:02:05,886
MATTHEW: His father, Matthew Griswold, became governor of Connecticut. His mother's father, Roger Wolcott, had been the colonial governor. Three generations, one family, one office.

14
00:02:06,186 --> 00:02:16,111
MATTHEW: He entered Yale at fourteen and graduated in 1780, during the Revolution. Then he read law, and the law led where it always led for a family like his. Into politics.

15
00:02:16,411 --> 00:02:22,843
THURSTON WHITMORE: Fourteen is young for Yale, I grant you. But the Griswolds were always early. To everything.

16
00:02:23,143 --> 00:02:25,717
GARY MERCER: Must be nice to have a reserved seat.

17
00:02:26,017 --> 00:02:37,901
MATTHEW: Griswold went to Congress in 1795, a Federalist, the party of merchants, bankers and old New England families. He was sharp, he was proud, and he did not like the new men from the frontier.

18
00:02:38,201 --> 00:02:56,655
MATTHEW: Matthew Lyon was the new man from the frontier. Born in Ireland in 1749. He came to Connecticut in 1764, around fifteen years old, as a redemptioner. That means he couldn't pay for the ship, so his labor was sold to cover it. He worked off that debt for years before he bought his freedom in 1768.

19
00:02:56,955 --> 00:03:12,747
MATTHEW: Lyon went north to Vermont, served with the Green Mountain Boys in the Revolution, built a gristmill, a sawmill and a paper mill, started a newspaper, and got himself elected to Congress. A bound laborer who became a congressman. To a Yale man, that was the insult.

20
00:03:13,047 --> 00:03:22,066
MATTHEW: After the brawl, a House committee recommended that both men be censured. The full House voted it down. Both men promised to keep the peace. Lyon apologized in writing.

21
00:03:22,366 --> 00:03:24,174
GARY MERCER: So nobody got punished.

22
00:03:24,474 --> 00:03:26,864
MATTHEW: Not that day, Gary. Wait for it.

23
00:03:27,164 --> 00:03:37,598
MATTHEW: Now, the counting. Connecticut's official list of governors goes all the way back to the colony's first governor, in 1639. On that list, Roger Griswold is number twenty-two.

24
00:03:37,898 --> 00:03:54,579
MATTHEW: But our game starts at independence. Jonathan Trumbull was governor when the colony became a state. Count from Trumbull, and you get Matthew Griswold, Samuel Huntington, Oliver Wolcott, Jonathan Trumbull Junior, John Treadwell, and then, number seven, Roger Griswold.

25
00:03:54,879 --> 00:04:00,878
THURSTON WHITMORE: Twenty-second or seventh. Either way, a Griswold. The club does keep its records.

26
00:04:01,178 --> 00:04:05,900
MATTHEW: Who gets counted is always a choice somebody made. Keep that in your pocket. It comes back all night.

27
00:04:06,200 --> 00:04:17,170
MATTHEW: Griswold was elected lieutenant governor in 1809 and governor in 1811. He was re-elected in the spring of 1812. And in June of 1812 the United States went to war with Britain.

28
00:04:17,470 --> 00:04:28,521
MATTHEW: New England's merchants hated that war. It strangled their trade. So when Washington asked Connecticut for militia, Governor Griswold said no. He refused to put the state's militia under federal command.

29
00:04:28,821 --> 00:04:38,118
MATTHEW: Remember, this is a man who, back in 1801, turned down John Adams when Adams offered to make him Secretary of War. He did not want to run the federal army. He wanted to keep his own.

30
00:04:38,418 --> 00:04:44,081
GARY MERCER: Hold on. He says no to being in charge of the war, then he says no to the war?

31
00:04:44,381 --> 00:04:49,065
THURSTON WHITMORE: Consistency, Gary. He simply preferred his militia at home. Near the silver.

32
00:04:49,365 --> 00:05:06,765
MATTHEW: So who was Matthew Lyon, really, by 1798? A mill owner. A printer whose paper was called The Farmers' Library. And when another paper wouldn't run his writing, he started one called The Scourge of Aristocracy. The Federalists nicknamed him the Spitting Lyon. To his voters, he was one of them.

33
00:05:07,065 --> 00:05:11,420
GARY MERCER: The Scourge of Aristocracy? Okay, now I get why they wanted him gone.

34
00:05:11,720 --> 00:05:21,643
MATTHEW: Now back to the man with the tongs. That summer of 1798, Congress passed the Sedition Act. It made it a crime to publish false, scandalous or malicious writing against the government.

35
00:05:21,943 --> 00:05:34,753
MATTHEW: That October, Matthew Lyon became the first person put on trial under it, for what he had written about President Adams. He was convicted on October 10th, fined a thousand dollars, and locked in a cell in Vergennes, Vermont, for four months.

36
00:05:35,053 --> 00:05:43,126
MATTHEW: And from that cell, the voters of Vermont re-elected him to Congress, four thousand five hundred seventy-six votes to two thousand four hundred forty-four.

37
00:05:43,426 --> 00:05:45,926
GARY MERCER: Okay. That's a heck of a comeback.

38
00:05:46,226 --> 00:05:57,393
MATTHEW: Same floor, same fight. The gentleman with the cane went home to Connecticut and kept rising. The servant with the tongs went to jail for what he wrote. One law for the club, another for the man who crashed it.

39
00:05:57,693 --> 00:06:05,511
MATTHEW: Roger Griswold did not get long in the governor's chair. He died in office on October 25th, 1812, in Norwich. He was fifty years old.

40
00:06:05,810 --> 00:06:20,614
MATTHEW: Before he ever sat in the governor's chair, Griswold sat on Connecticut's highest court, from 1807 to 1809. He was a presidential elector for the Federalist ticket of Charles Cotesworth Pinckney and Rufus King. Bench, ballot, and a seat at every table that mattered.

41
00:06:20,914 --> 00:06:35,160
MATTHEW: And there's one more thing on his record. In 1803, when Thomas Jefferson bought Louisiana, Griswold was one of the New England Federalists who talked about taking New England out of the Union. Their region was losing power to the South and the West, and they did not like it.

42
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:40,468
GARY MERCER: Wait. The governor who wouldn't send his militia to the war also wanted to leave the country?

43
00:06:40,768 --> 00:06:45,343
THURSTON WHITMORE: Gary, a gentleman never leaves. He simply considers other clubs.

44
00:06:45,643 --> 00:06:55,101
MATTHEW: Remember that one, because two of our seven governors were in that same secession conversation in 1803. You'll meet the other one in New Hampshire. He lived long enough to call it a mistake.

45
00:06:55,401 --> 00:07:08,050
MATTHEW: At home, Griswold married Fanny Rogers, and they had ten children. His grandson, another Matthew Griswold, went to Congress from Pennsylvania in the 1890s. In this family, public office was the family business.

46
00:07:08,350 --> 00:07:11,652
THURSTON WHITMORE: Ten children. Good heavens. Think of the tuition.

47
00:07:11,952 --> 00:07:13,434
GARY MERCER: Think of the canes.

48
00:07:13,734 --> 00:07:19,911
MATTHEW: Three years later, Connecticut named a town for him. Griswold, Connecticut, incorporated in 1815, out in the eastern hills.

49
00:07:20,211 --> 00:07:26,928
MATTHEW: Yale gave him an honorary law degree in 1812, the year he died. His grave is in the family cemetery at Black Hall, in Old Lyme.

50
00:07:27,228 --> 00:07:33,404
THURSTON WHITMORE: A Yale man, a Yale father, a governor's grandson. Boola boola, I suppose.

51
00:07:33,704 --> 00:07:39,164
MATTHEW: A town for the man with the cane. A jail cell for the man with the tongs. Connecticut's lucky number seven.

52
00:07:39,464 --> 00:07:42,438
MATTHEW: So, Connecticut. Your lucky seven, one more time.

53
00:07:42,738 --> 00:07:48,083
MATTHEW: One. In 1798 Roger Griswold attacked a congressman with a cane on the House floor.

54
00:07:48,383 --> 00:07:51,947
MATTHEW: Two. His father and his grandfather were both governors of Connecticut.

55
00:07:52,247 --> 00:07:57,370
MATTHEW: Three. Matthew Lyon came to America owing for his passage and worked it off as a bound laborer.

56
00:07:57,670 --> 00:08:02,102
MATTHEW: Four. Counted from independence, Roger Griswold is Connecticut's seventh governor.

57
00:08:02,402 --> 00:08:07,666
MATTHEW: Five. As governor in 1812 he refused to put Connecticut's militia under federal control.

58
00:08:07,966 --> 00:08:13,567
MATTHEW: Six. Lyon was the first person tried under the Sedition Act. Four months in jail, a $1,000 fine.

59
00:08:13,867 --> 00:08:18,660
MATTHEW: Seven. The town of Griswold, Connecticut, incorporated in 1815, is named for him.

60
00:08:18,960 --> 00:08:21,059
MATTHEW: Connecticut. Keep it real.
